WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.325
The one idea everything else rests on.

2
00:00:02.605 --> 00:00:07.597
A large language model does one thing: given some text, it predicts what comes next.

3
00:00:07.877 --> 00:00:15.600
More precisely, it reads a sequence of tokens and outputs a probability for every token in its vocabulary being the next one.

4
00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:20.915
The application picks one token from that distribution, appends it, and asks again.

5
00:00:21.195 --> 00:00:24.608
A paragraph of output is this loop run a few hundred times.

6
00:00:24.888 --> 00:00:31.139
That framing sounds too simple to explain what these models can do, and it is worth holding two things at once.

7
00:00:31.419 --> 00:00:34.128
The mechanism really is next-token prediction.

8
00:00:34.408 --> 00:00:44.605
And predicting the next token well, across trillions of words of human writing, requires the model to pick up grammar, facts, styles, and some ability to follow reasoning.

9
00:00:44.885 --> 00:00:52.352
Most of what you will build in this program sits on top of that loop, so understanding it pays off every time an output surprises you.
